<section class="section section--line" id="story" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
  <div class="container split">
    <div class="split__sticky reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow__dot"></span>Our story</span>
      <h2 id="story-heading">Fixing the CRM trust gap</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-lg reveal" style="--d:1">
      <p>Our founder, Allen Trinidad, spent years as a HubSpot subject-matter expert supporting HubSpot Partner agencies, then went independent. The same three sentences came up on almost every project.</p>
      <p>From founders: <em>"we bought HubSpot and we are still working in spreadsheets."</em> From sales leaders: <em>"I do not trust the forecast."</em> From marketing: <em>"we cannot tell you which campaigns produced revenue."</em></p>
      <p>None of those are HubSpot problems. They are what happens when a CRM gets configured quickly, never architected, and never documented. The platform is capable. It was just never set up to match how the business actually runs.</p>
      <p>PipelineSync was founded in 2025 to fix that specific problem. Not an agency selling campaigns, but a technical consultancy focused on CRM architecture, data quality, automation, and reporting. Five years of hands-on HubSpot work, and more than 30 businesses later, the pattern is still the same one we started with.</p>
      <blockquote class="pullquote"><p>"We work directly with you, not through layers, to build a CRM your team trusts and uses every day."</p><cite>Allen Trinidad, Founder</cite></blockquote>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <div class="section-head section-head--center reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow__dot"></span>Our team</span>
      <h2 id="team-heading">Eight people, one platform</h2>
      <p>PipelineSync is a team of eight: our founder, five HubSpot implementation specialists, and two supporting social media and administrative staff. Everyone who touches client work is on our team. We do not subcontract builds.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stats reveal" data-inview>
      <div class="stat"><div class="stat__num" data-count="8">8</div><div class="stat__label">People on the team</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="stat__num" data-count="5">5</div><div class="stat__label">HubSpot specialists</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="stat__num" data-count="0">0</div><div class="stat__label">Subcontracted builds</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="stat__num" data-count="24" data-suffix="h">24h</div><div class="stat__label">Reply time, business days</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-lg mt-lg reveal">
      <p>We are based in the Philippines and work US business hours. That structure is deliberate. It gives clients senior HubSpot capability at a rate that a US or UK agency with the same depth cannot match, and it means work moves on your calendar, not ours.</p>
      <p>There is a second reason we built the team this way. The Philippines has a large pool of skilled remote professionals whose work is undervalued because the roles available to them are undervalued. We train ours into genuine HubSpot specialists, with certifications, real project experience, and career progression. Our clients get people who are invested in the platform rather than passing through it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--line" aria-labelledby="diff-heading">
